faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The pc0→pc12 path was tested live (Phase 1) and shows 0% packet loss in both directions with sub-millisecond RTT. The routing architecture is a deliberate OSPF/EIGRP hybrid: the backbone (r1 through r9) runs OSPF, while the r12/r13 segment runs EIGRP AS 100. Router r11 acts as the redistribution boundary, running both protocols simultaneously — OSPF on eth1 (111.x.x.x, area 3, connected to r1), and EIGRP on eth0 (112.x.x.x, connected to r12). r11's OSPF block contains `redistribute eigrp`, which injects r12's directly-connected 120.0.0.0/8 network into OSPF so that r1 correctly installs `O>* 120.0.0.0/8 via 111.1.1.2`. The return path from pc12 is handled by a kernel default route on r12 (`K>* 0.0.0.0/0 via 112.1.1.1`) pointing back to r11, which then routes to 20.0.0.0/8 via OSPF. The OSPF snapshot flagged r12 (ospfd running with no network statements) and r13 (ospfd absent) — both are expected artefacts of the EIGRP-only segment. A real OSPF misconfiguration exists on r8 (area 0.0.0.2 is orphaned with 0 Full adjacencies across 5 interfaces), but r8 lies on a completely separate branch (r5→r8 on the W segment) and has no bearing on the pc0→pc12 forwarding path. No flap scripts, link-down history, resource pressure, or DHCP anomalies were found on any device. The user's report of total connectivity loss to pc12 is not reproduced by live measurement and cannot be confirmed; it most likely reflects a transient during the lab's initial OSPF/EIGRP convergence window.
=== END ===